WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.171
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got a robotic camera that zooms fifty times and IDs three hundred bird species mid-shot, a century-old museum frog that just became its own species, and a caterpillar that hears without ears. Plus the Deep Dive: is any Cariboo community actually self-sufficient, or does it just run on networks nobody notices? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:44.882
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Sunday, September 6th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World.

00:00:45.049 --> 00:00:47.562
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're looking at what science actually reveals about the world around us — wildfire smoke, childhood illness, a frog nobody noticed for a century, and a caterpillar that hears without ears.

00:00:47.612 --> 00:00:48.976
<v Riley>Fifteen stories on the list today, a strong mix of local news and global research. Let's get into it.

00:00:50.377 --> 00:00:55.248
<v Riley>More than three thousand four hundred Williams Lake and area residents lost power on Thursday.

00:00:55.475 --> 00:01:01.645
<v Casey>Nine separate outages, three of them reported that same day. That's a system under strain, not one bad transformer.

00:01:01.873 --> 00:01:16.486
<v Riley>BC Hydro hasn't given a public cause for most of them. A smaller, separate outage south of Little Fort along Highway 24 knocked out power to 320 customers on its own, and six other smaller outages scattered through the South Cariboo hit about 80 more.

00:01:17.396 --> 00:01:31.035
<v Riley>Different note. The Hooligans motorcycle club held their annual charity ride Saturday, supporting the Williams Lake Boys and Girls Club. Riders geared up at the start line, rode a set route, and raised money that goes straight to the club's youth programming.

00:01:31.263 --> 00:01:37.758
<v Casey>Small in scale, but that's the fundraiser that quietly outlasts flashier campaigns because the same riders show up every year.

00:01:37.985 --> 00:01:47.078
<v Riley>The Tribune's photo coverage showed member Robert Oddie gearing up before the ride — this is the seventh year the club's run it, by the sound of things.

00:01:47.987 --> 00:02:05.199
<v Casey>Sticking with community infrastructure — the Williams Lake library branch is closed for renovations, and pop-up library services are opening right outside the building so people can still borrow books while the work is underway. The branch is set to reopen November 3rd, with the pop-up running from September 8th through October 16th.

00:02:05.426 --> 00:02:12.896
<v Riley>A pop-up is a decent stopgap. A library that just goes dark for months loses patrons who don't come back once it reopens.

00:02:13.805 --> 00:02:31.342
<v Casey>On September 11th, thirteen first responders set out from Fort St. John on the Cops for Cancer Tour de North — eight hundred fifty kilometres by bike across northern B.C. They're expected to reach Williams Lake around September 17th, stopping at schools and communities the whole way to raise money for childhood cancer research.

00:02:31.569 --> 00:02:38.389
<v Riley>Eight hundred fifty kilometres in a week, for a cause that gets a fraction of the attention adult cancer research does.

00:02:39.298 --> 00:02:50.664
<v Riley>The Bonanza Creek wildfire near Clinton has been reclassified as "being held" — the BC Wildfire Service's term for a fire crews don't expect to grow past its current perimeter under existing conditions and resources.

00:02:50.892 --> 00:02:58.036
<v Casey>Held isn't out. Anyone still under an evacuation alert there should keep checking, not assume the danger's passed because the classification changed.

00:02:58.263 --> 00:03:06.057
<v Riley>True. The fire burned long enough to prompt evacuation orders earlier this summer, so this is a step down in status, not a close-out.

00:03:06.967 --> 00:03:34.571
<v Casey>The Williams Lake Tribune ran a longer piece this week tracing how the B.C. Conservatives went from a rising force in the legislature to a party dealing with open turmoil. It traces back to when former leader John Rustad expelled Vancouver-Quilchena MLA Dallas Brodie in March of 2025 over social media comments about residential schools. Since then, more MLAs — including Kelowna-Lake Country-Coldstream's Tara Armstrong — have left the caucus. Current leader Kerry-Lynne Findlay is now managing what's left of it, according to the piece.

00:03:34.798 --> 00:03:41.942
<v Riley>That kind of fracture in a party with seats in this region matters for anyone wondering how Cariboo ridings get represented provincially.

00:03:42.852 --> 00:03:54.867
<v Casey>North Thompson fire crews — out of Barriere, Clearwater, and the surrounding area — spent a difficult wildfire season this summer deploying to other B.C. communities that needed extra hands when their own local fires demanded it.

00:03:55.095 --> 00:04:00.291
<v Riley>Mutual aid between departments is the arrangement nobody notices until the week it saves a town.

00:04:00.518 --> 00:04:08.637
<v Casey>The Barriere fire chief described it as routine for them now — when a neighbouring community calls, crews go, understanding the favour runs both ways.

00:04:09.546 --> 00:04:18.639
<v Riley>Last close-to-home item. Overnight traffic pattern changes have been underway on Highway 1 westbound at Mount Lehman Road near Abbotsford since August 31st, part of ongoing interchange construction.

00:04:18.867 --> 00:04:25.037
<v Casey>Not a Cariboo story, but if you're driving to the coast this month, budget extra time for that stretch.

00:04:25.946 --> 00:04:41.859
<v Riley>Now the science stories, starting with the one that made us both laugh a little reading it. Former DJI engineers have built what they're calling the world's first robotic extreme-zoom camera. Fifty-times zoom, AI-powered subject tracking, and it can identify more than three hundred bird species while it's shooting.

00:04:42.086 --> 00:04:45.983
<v Casey>Scout Island's birders are going to want a demo of that thing.

00:04:46.211 --> 00:05:01.799
<v Riley>It's aimed at wildlife photographers who need to hold a shot on a bird at real distance without spooking it off the branch. Given how much birding culture already runs through places like Scout Island, that could plausibly end up in local hands within a couple of years.

00:05:02.708 --> 00:05:14.074
<v Casey>Johns Hopkins University researchers, working with Yayuan Liu's battery lab and the Toyota Research Institute, are developing alternatives to lithium batteries — solid-state, iron-air, and zinc-manganese oxide chemistries, aimed at safer, more sustainable energy storage.

00:05:14.302 --> 00:05:25.343
<v Riley>Iron-air keeps coming up as a candidate for long-duration grid storage. Remembering Williams Lake just had nine outages in one report, that's exactly the kind of research that eventually turns into something useful here.

00:05:26.252 --> 00:05:40.217
<v Casey>Scientific American had a piece on why your ability to cope with stress changes with the seasons. Daylight exposure and ambient temperature shift cortisol regulation and mood more than most people assume — measurable biology, not just a vibe about winter being hard.

00:05:41.126 --> 00:05:56.714
<v Riley>WIRED covered a study where researchers put caterpillars inside an ultraquiet chamber to figure out how they detect sound without ears at all. Fine hairs on their bodies sense vibration in the air, and the mechanism is precise enough that engineers think it could inform better microphone design.

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:12.237
<v Casey>And a frog that's been sitting in museum collections since the 1800s just got formally recognized as its own species. It's common across Ecuador and Colombia, mistaken for a similar relative for more than a century, until researchers compared old specimens against new field samples.

00:06:12.464 --> 00:06:18.959
<v Riley>A reminder that undiscovered species aren't always deep in a jungle nobody's been. Sometimes they're already in a drawer, mislabeled.

00:06:19.869 --> 00:06:28.962
<v Riley>Fast Company ran a piece on America's growing disposable workforce — companies leaning harder on contract and gig labour, drawn from a new book by MIT's Paul Osterman.

00:06:29.189 --> 00:06:38.282
<v Casey>Part of what's driving that, per the piece, is uncertainty about AI's effect on jobs. Nobody wants to commit to headcount they might not need in two years.

00:06:39.191 --> 00:06:48.609
<v Riley>Last item — an arthritis drug is restoring up to a hundred percent of hair growth in patients with severe alopecia, according to new trial results reported this week.

00:06:48.836 --> 00:06:57.930
<v Casey>An existing, already-approved drug repurposed for a different condition. Cheaper and faster than inventing something new. We'll come back to that idea of repurposing in the Deep Dive.

00:06:58.611 --> 00:07:01.209
<v Riley>That's the roundup. Time for today's Community Spotlight.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:33.184
<v Casey>Today we're highlighting Scout Island Nature Centre, the volunteer-run education centre operated by the Williams Lake Field Naturalists. They run birding walks, kids' programs, and a Salmon in the Classroom initiative that puts real fish eggs in front of local students. Right now they're taking applications for the Salmon Trip — a three-day outing at Gavin Lake where students help with brood stock collection and stream surveys, hands-on salmon habitat research, not a classroom simulation. Applications are open at scoutisland-dot-c-a.

00:07:33.452 --> 00:07:42.624
<v Riley>A program run entirely by volunteers that puts kids in an actual stream doing fieldwork — worth applying for before the spots fill up.

00:07:42.892 --> 00:07:44.420
<v Casey>Onto the Deep Dive.

00:07:45.821 --> 00:08:12.077
<v Riley>Start with the wildfire smoke piece. Researchers describe smoke particles staying suspended for weeks and traveling thousands of kilometers from the burn. Canada's 2024 fire season was early and unusually intense — over a hundred and thirty separate blazes, per the BBC — and smoke reached cities nowhere near the fires themselves. Nobody in a city gets to opt out of that by being self-sufficient about their own air.

00:08:12.719 --> 00:08:30.535
<v Casey>The framing that "wildfires moved into the city" risks flattening who bears the worst of it, though. A city dweller gets a bad air quality week and maybe buys a purifier. Somebody in Clinton this summer got the fire itself, an evacuation order, and the smoke on the drive out. Those aren't the same event dressed up differently.

00:08:31.213 --> 00:08:47.357
<v Riley>The EPA and peer-reviewed meta-analyses do confirm smoke exposure raises all-cause mortality and respiratory hospitalizations well beyond the fire's immediate radius. That's a measurable health effect extending outward, and it's growing as fire seasons get longer.

00:08:47.957 --> 00:09:08.309
<v Casey>Measurable at what distance, though. A hospitalization spike in a city far from the nearest burn is a different order of harm than what a fire-adjacent community absorbs directly. If attention and dollars flow toward urban air-quality fixes — purifiers, PSA campaigns — because that's where the population and the headlines are, the frontline community gets less.

00:09:08.905 --> 00:09:24.745
<v Riley>That's the real question underneath all this. Can a community handle its own fire risk on its own terms, or does it only survive because it's plugged into something bigger — provincial fire crews, shared air monitoring, an evacuation network reaching past the fire line?

00:09:25.003 --> 00:09:39.051
<v Casey>North Thompson crews deploying out of Barriere and Clearwater to other B.C. communities this summer answers that. Nobody up here fights fire alone. The model is mutual aid between departments that would otherwise each be undersized for a bad season.

00:09:39.629 --> 00:09:57.525
<v Riley>And the "being held" classification on Bonanza Creek only means something because it's tied to provincial resource allocation. Being held means crews and equipment are still assigned, watching, ready to respond if wind shifts. That's a shared resourcing decision made somewhere outside Clinton.

00:09:58.171 --> 00:10:19.875
<v Casey>Same logic applies to the childhood dementia piece, and there the science is thinner than the headline suggests. The condition being described is almost certainly Batten disease — the leading cause of dementia in children. A gene therapy trial is already underway, delivering treatment directly into the spinal fluid, still in early dose-escalation safety testing years after it started.

00:10:20.545 --> 00:10:25.929
<v Riley>So "we've found a way to effectively treat it" is doing more than the underlying research supports.

00:10:26.447 --> 00:10:45.983
<v Casey>Considerably more. What's getting attention now is different from that gene therapy trial — it's a stem cell drug screen, testing already-approved drugs against patient-derived cells in a dish to see which show activity against the disease. That's checking an existing library of compounds that already cleared safety trials for something else entirely.

00:10:46.583 --> 00:11:00.479
<v Riley>Which is the smart move if you're trying to move fast. Repurposing shortcuts years of toxicology work a novel drug would need. If something in that library already has an established safety profile in adults, you're not starting from zero.

00:11:01.135 --> 00:11:17.023
<v Casey>Granted, and it's still a lab-dish result. The gene therapy trial for the same disease has run for years without reaching approval. "Effectively treat" in a headline and an approved therapy a family can actually get for their kid are a decade apart, maybe more, going by that timeline.

00:11:17.616 --> 00:11:39.040
<v Riley>But the structural point holds regardless of pace. This lead only exists because there's a shared, catalogued library of approved drugs to screen against. A single lab trying to invent a cure from scratch, with no access to that catalogue, doesn't find this lead at all. The infrastructure that makes the finding possible stays invisible in the headline.

00:11:39.568 --> 00:11:48.872
<v Casey>Nobody's self-sufficient in a drug pipeline. Every promising lead stands on decades of other institutions' chemistry, most of it developed for a completely different disease.

00:11:49.432 --> 00:11:57.656
<v Riley>Then there's the alcohol study, and this is the one where the failure isn't in the science at all. It's in the signal reaching anybody's kitchen table.

00:11:58.182 --> 00:12:16.782
<v Casey>Alcohol-attributable cancer deaths in the U.S. nearly doubled between 1990 and 2023 — from eleven thousand four hundred to twenty-three thousand one hundred, per the University of Miami-led study. The rise shows up across age groups and multiple cancer types, not just one. Population growth doesn't explain it away.

00:12:17.419 --> 00:12:26.003
<v Riley>And separately, MD Anderson found just over half of American adults don't know alcohol affects cancer risk at all — fifty-two point nine percent.

00:12:26.553 --> 00:12:35.721
<v Casey>The science has been solid for years. The network supposed to carry that information from the study to the person deciding whether to have a second drink is what's broken.

00:12:36.341 --> 00:12:46.333
<v Riley>Different failure from the smoke framing — that message got flattened on the way out, but it still went out. This is worse. For more than half the country, the message never left the building.

00:12:47.019 --> 00:13:05.211
<v Casey>Which raises the harder question. Communication failure, or a public health system that quietly decided this fight wasn't worth having? Tobacco warnings took decades of deliberate labeling, advertising restrictions, and a coordinated campaign to move public awareness that far. Nobody's mounted an equivalent effort for alcohol.

00:13:05.738 --> 00:13:20.330
<v Riley>There's a real risk in trying, though. Frame alcohol with the same blunt intensity as tobacco messaging, and you risk the fatigue that already undercuts public health communication — the shrug where everything supposedly causes cancer, so nothing does.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:30.910
<v Casey>Maybe. But there's no message loud enough yet to provoke a shrug. Fifty-three percent unaware isn't a message that's been tried and worn thin. It's a message that's barely shipped.

00:13:31.427 --> 00:13:36.683
<v Riley>So what would actually move the number, if a blunt tobacco-style campaign risks backfiring?

00:13:36.983 --> 00:13:50.375
<v Casey>Probably something closer to what changed drunk driving attitudes — not "alcohol is poison," a specific number tied to something people already track. A doctor mentioning it at an annual physical does more than a poster in a liquor store.

00:13:51.275 --> 00:14:19.403
<v Riley>Bring this back to here specifically. Interior Health issues air quality advisories during fire season based on monitoring stations that feed into a provincial network — the same connective tissue that let North Thompson crews know where to send trucks this summer. Williams Lake doesn't run its own independent smoke-detection system. It relies on a shared network, the same way dementia research relies on a shared drug library it didn't build itself.

00:14:19.962 --> 00:14:34.938
<v Casey>And a city campfire ban only means anything because it's coordinated with the regional wildfire service's fire danger rating. The city isn't deciding independently that conditions are dangerous. It's responding to a number generated somewhere else and applied locally.

00:14:35.515 --> 00:14:46.483
<v Riley>So even something that looks like local self-governance — a city banning campfires within its own limits — is downstream of a provincial data network telling them the threshold got crossed.

00:14:47.103 --> 00:15:06.775
<v Casey>Which is the pattern running through all three stories today. Smoke crosses borders nobody drew for that purpose. A drug lead only exists because of a shared library built for other diseases entirely. Cancer risk stays invisible to half the public because the network meant to carry that finding stalled somewhere between the lab and the kitchen table.

00:15:07.419 --> 00:15:21.683
<v Riley>The self-sufficient story people tell about a place like this is often a story about infrastructure they've stopped noticing — the monitoring station, the drug approval process, the fire dispatch line that answers when Barriere calls.

00:15:22.318 --> 00:15:41.206
<v Casey>I'd stop short of saying that covers everything. There are things a community handles better on its own — a volunteer crew's read on which slope catches wind first, knowledge that never shows up in a provincial database. But none of today's three stories are examples of that. They're examples of the network mattering more than people assume.

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:58.574
<v Riley>So what's the concrete move for a place like Williams Lake or Quesnel? Not "build more infrastructure" — that funding conversation is worn thin. The real question is which of these networks the region has visibility into, versus which ones it just trusts blindly because nobody's asked.

00:15:59.188 --> 00:16:15.884
<v Casey>Does anyone locally know which drugs are actually in that stem cell screen, or is that a press release we'll read again in five years when a trial finally opens? Does Interior Health publish which monitoring stations feed the Cariboo's air quality number, or is that a black box reading on an app nobody's audited?

00:16:16.195 --> 00:16:29.603
<v Riley>That's worth putting to Interior Health directly — where the nearest monitoring station to Williams Lake sits, and whether smoke readings from a fire near Clinton get folded into the same regional number or reported separately.

00:16:30.252 --> 00:16:38.868
<v Casey>If a listener works in public health, or with Interior Health, and knows the answer, that beats a guess from either of us — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:16:39.532 --> 00:16:49.460
<v Riley>Not who pays for the network, then — who actually knows how it's built, and whether that knowledge is public or just assumed to exist. Have a good rest of your Sunday.

00:16:50.048 --> 00:16:55.144
<v Casey>We'll be back tomorrow. Try not to think too hard about your air quality app in the meantime.